title: CEL policy expressions, hands-on
guide: howto/cel-policy-expressions.html
promise: Common Expression Language (CEL) for cloud security: macros, the has() rule that decides fail open or closed, a real Kubernetes admission policy.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
It is for intermediate readers who know Kubernetes basics, such as Pods, one or more containers run together. CEL is taught from the start.

You need Python 3.10 or later. Docker and kind are only for a throwaway cluster.

== How the guide was checked
section: How to use this guide
A script ran the code blocks on 4 November 2026, with cel-python 0.5.0; the outputs shown are from that run.

Not run: step 6's condition in Google Cloud IAM, and any gcloud command. What Google does comes from its documentation.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
Paste BASH blocks into your terminal whole and at once. Steps 2 and 3 run expressions through cel.py, step 1's evaluator. Save a YAML block only when the text says to. CEL blocks are to read; unlabelled blocks are output.

== Set up your environment
section: 1. Get an evaluator you can iterate in
Step 1's block makes ~/cel-lab, installs cel-python 0.5.0, a CEL implementation in Python, and saves a sample Pod as pod.json. It ends by writing cel.py, which evaluates expressions against pod.json, and running a first check. Use Python 3.10 or later.
```bash
cat > cel.py <<'EOF'
import os, sys, json, celpy
env = celpy.Environment()
doc = celpy.json_to_cel(json.load(open(os.environ.get('DOC', 'pod.json'))))
for src in sys.argv[1:]:
    print(f"{src}\n  -> {env.program(env.compile(src)).evaluate(doc)}\n")
EOF

python3 cel.py 'object.spec.replicas <= 5'
```

== What CEL is and where you meet it
section: The policy language that lives inside the thing it governs
CEL is a small language for single-expression rules, each true or false, such as "every container must set a memory limit".

It has no loops, changes nothing and always finishes, so Kubernetes and Google Cloud run it inside their servers.

== The one idea: a missing field is an error
section: What a CEL policy is made of
The variable object holds the resource being checked. Reading a missing field is an error, not false; in Kubernetes, failurePolicy, the setting that says whether an error blocks the request or lets it through, decides. Test presence with has() first.
```cel
// Errors, if any container omits securityContext:
object.spec.containers.all(c, c.securityContext.runAsNonRoot == true)

// Correct: presence-check first. && short-circuits, so the right-hand
// side is never evaluated when has() is false.
object.spec.containers.all(c,
  has(c.securityContext) && c.securityContext.runAsNonRoot == true)
```

== The syntax, in one pass
section: 2. The syntax, in one pass
CEL is C-like and mostly unsurprising. Strings take double or single quotes; the in operator tests map keys and list membership; the ternary a ? b : c gives b when a is true and c otherwise.
```bash
python3 cel.py \
  'object.metadata.name == "web"' \
  'object.spec.replicas > 5 && object.spec.replicas < 20' \
  '"team" in object.metadata.labels' \
  'object.metadata.labels["team"] == "payments"' \
  'object.metadata.name.startsWith("web")' \
  'size(object.spec.containers) == 2' \
  'object.spec.replicas > 5 ? "too many" : "ok"'
```

== Macros: where real policies live
section: 3. Macros, which is where real policies live
Macros are CEL's only iteration: all, exists, exists_one, filter and map, each over a collection you already have. In pod.json, the container named sidecar has no securityContext and no resources, and pulls :latest, so it fails three of these five.
```bash
python3 cel.py \
  'object.spec.containers.all(c, has(c.resources) && has(c.resources.limits))' \
  'object.spec.containers.exists(c, c.image.endsWith(":latest"))' \
  'object.spec.containers.map(c, c.name)' \
  'size(object.spec.containers.filter(c, !has(c.securityContext)))' \
  'object.spec.containers.all(c, c.image.startsWith("ghcr.io/acme/"))'
```

== Step 4: a real ValidatingAdmissionPolicy
section: 4. Write a real ValidatingAdmissionPolicy
Admission control checks each request to create, change or delete a Kubernetes object. This policy, saved in step 5 as policy.yaml, matches Deployments; failurePolicy: Fail means an evaluation error denies the request. A second validation refuses :latest tags.
```yaml
  validations:
    - expression: >-
        object.spec.template.spec.containers.all(c,
          has(c.resources) && has(c.resources.limits) &&
          has(c.resources.limits.memory))
      message: "every container must set a memory limit"
```

== A binding turns the policy on
section: 4. Write a real ValidatingAdmissionPolicy
The policy does nothing until a binding applies it. Step 5's policy.yaml holds this one and a Deny binding for production namespaces. Bind as [Warn, Audit] first, read the audit log, then add Deny.
```yaml
apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding
metadata:
  name: "require-resource-limits-dev.csoh.org"
spec:
  policyName: "require-resource-limits.csoh.org"
  validationActions: [Warn, Audit]   # same rule, observe-only in dev
  matchResources:
    namespaceSelector:
      matchLabels:
        environment: development
```

== Step 5: try it on a throwaway cluster
section: 5. Try it on a throwaway cluster
This needs Docker and kind. Save step 4's policy and both bindings as policy.yaml, with a --- line between the policy and the first binding. The create deployment line should be refused with "every container must set a memory limit".
```bash
kind create cluster --name cel-lab
kubectl apply -f policy.yaml

kubectl create namespace prod
kubectl label namespace prod environment=production

kubectl -n prod create deployment bad --image=nginx:latest

kind delete cluster --name cel-lab
```

== Same CEL, other variables: CRDs and IAM
section: 6. Two other places the same expressions turn up
A CustomResourceDefinition (CRD) adds a resource type to a cluster, and CEL on its schema rejects an invalid one on write. Google Cloud IAM conditions use the same language with completely different variables, here for a grant that expires:
```cel
// Grant expires on 1 September 2026, for buckets named acme-backups*.
request.time < timestamp("2026-09-01T00:00:00Z") &&
resource.type == "storage.googleapis.com/Bucket" &&
resource.name.startsWith("projects/_/buckets/acme-backups")
```

== A Google Cloud constraint does nothing alone
section: Two objects, and the first one does nothing
A custom constraint is a rule with a CEL condition; save this one as bucket-pap.yaml. Like a policy with no binding, it does nothing until an organization policy turns it on. ALLOW passes only requests that make the condition true.
```yaml
name: organizations/ORGANIZATION_ID/customConstraints/custom.bucketRequirePap
resourceTypes:
- storage.googleapis.com/Bucket
methodTypes:
- CREATE
- UPDATE
condition: "resource.iamConfiguration.publicAccessPrevention == 'enforced'"
actionType: ALLOW
displayName: Buckets must enforce public access prevention
description: Set public access prevention to enforced on this bucket.
```

== Evaluate the condition before Google does
section: Evaluate the condition before Google does
These end a block that saves bucket-ok.json and bucket-open.json, one compliant and one not; DOC tells cel.py which to read. Expect True, then False. Locally a missing key is an error even where Google would read a typed field's default.
```bash
DOC=bucket-ok.json python3 cel.py "resource.iamConfiguration.publicAccessPrevention == 'enforced'"
DOC=bucket-open.json python3 cel.py "resource.iamConfiguration.publicAccessPrevention == 'enforced'"
```

== Test, then enforce
section: Test, then enforce
The guide did not run these: they need a Google Cloud organization and the right to change its policies. The block first writes bucket-pap-dryrun.yaml and bucket-pap-live.yaml. Policy Simulator lists existing violations; a dry-run policy logs violations and denies nothing.
```bash
gcloud policy-intelligence simulate orgpolicy \
  --organization=ORGANIZATION_ID \
  --custom-constraints=bucket-pap.yaml \
  --policies=bucket-pap-live.yaml
gcloud org-policies set-custom-constraint bucket-pap.yaml
gcloud org-policies set-policy bucket-pap-dryrun.yaml
gcloud org-policies set-policy bucket-pap-live.yaml --update-mask=spec
```

== Exercise: what does a missing field give?
section: Hands-on exercises
What does object.spec.nodeName == "" evaluate to, given that pod.json has no nodeName? Predict first, then run it with cel.py. The guide hides each answer until you open "Show the answer".

== Answer: an error, and failurePolicy decides
section: Hands-on exercises
It is an error, not false, and it propagates out of the whole expression. With failurePolicy: Fail, a typo in a field name blocks every matching deployment; with Ignore, it silently disables the control. One correct form is has(object.spec.nodeName).

== Exercise: a DENY that reads a missing label
section: Hands-on exercises
A colleague's constraint uses actionType: DENY, which blocks a request when the condition is true, to stop buckets labelled exposure: public. What does it do in Google Cloud to a bucket with no exposure label? Run it, then fix it.
```bash
DOC=bucket-ok.json python3 cel.py "resource.labels['exposure'] == 'public'"
```

== Answer: test for the key first
section: Hands-on exercises
Locally it is an error. In Google Cloud a missing map key is an error, and it blocks the request: every bucket without an exposure label is refused. Test the key first; False means the DENY lets the bucket through.
```bash
DOC=bucket-ok.json python3 cel.py "'exposure' in resource.labels && resource.labels['exposure'] == 'public'"
```

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- Reading an optional field without has() hands the outcome to failurePolicy.
- A CREATE-only constraint never sees a later update.
- Require the good value with ALLOW: a DENY on the bad value may miss requests that leave the field out.
